      {
        "id": "pipeline",
        "title": "Tracing a primitive through the graphics processor",
        "source": "CGVIZ_Chapter_1_2026.pdf p. 1–31 · CGVIZ_Chapter_5_2026.pdf p. 3–9, 12–23 and 55–62",
        "html": "<p>A vertex has a position and may have attributes such as colour and a normal. Indices group vertices into primitives. The vertex stage expresses positions in clip space. Clipping may create new vertices along the boundary of the view volume. Raster positions are obtained only after perspective division and the viewport transformation.</p><p>Rasterisation finds covered samples and interpolates attributes. A fragment computation proposes a colour and depth. The depth test determines whether the sample may affect the image. The vertex count alone therefore does not determine the workload: one large triangle can cover the entire screen.</p><p>A quadrilateral is described by four vertices and the triangle indices (0,1,2), (0,2,3). Local position number 2 is shared by both triangles and receives the same transformation. A flat surface may nevertheless need separate vertex records at a sharp discontinuity in normals or texture coordinates.</p>\n<details class=\"sjekk\"><summary>Basic practice · What is the difference between a vertex and a fragment?</summary><p>Follow the triangle through to rasterisation.</p><details><summary>Show answer</summary><p>A vertex describes a geometric position with attributes. Rasterisation can produce many fragments from the three vertices, with interpolated values at the covered samples.</p></details></details>\n<div class=\"exercise\"><strong>Application · our practice exercise</strong><p>Two triangles have the same three screen positions. How can their results still differ?</p><details><summary>Hint</summary><p>Distinguish positions before rasterisation from attributes and buffer contents.</p></details><details><summary>Show answer</summary><p>They may have different depths, materials or vertex colours. Rasterisation then covers the same samples, while interpolation, lighting and the depth test can produce different visible colours.</p></details></div>"
      },
      {
        "id": "hierarki",
        "title": "From local geometry to the world",
        "source": "SceneGraphs.pdf § 9.1–9.2, PDF p. 1–5 · CGVIZ_Chapter_9_2020.pdf p. 2–12",
        "html": "\n<p>A vertex describes one position. Three vertices can form a triangle primitive. A vertex table and an index list let several triangles share the same vertex data. A <dfn>scene graph</dfn> connects this geometry to groups and transformations.</p>\n<p>Let A be the matrix from arm space to world space, and H the matrix from hand space to arm space. Then p<sub>world</sub> = A H p<sub>hand</sub>. A sibling branch for the other hand must start from the same A. During traversal, you can save A on a stack, visit the hand, then restore A.</p>\n<p><strong>Example.</strong> H translates two units along local x. A rotates by +90° about z, then translates by (4, 0). The hand's origin (0, 0) becomes (2, 0) in arm space, (0, 2) after rotation, and (4, 2) in world space. Check: its distance from the arm's world position (4, 0) is still 2.</p>\n<aside class=\"worked-example\" lang=\"en\"><h3>Trace a scene graph</h3><p>A child is translated two units along its parent’s local x-axis. The parent rotates 90° counterclockwise about the world origin. To locate the child’s origin, first express it in parent coordinates, then convert those coordinates to world coordinates.</p>\n<div class=\"calculation\">(0, 0) → (2, 0) → (0, 2)</div><p>Where does the child's local point (1, 0) end up?</p><details><summary>Check your reasoning</summary><p>Adding the local offset gives (3, 0) in parent space. Rotating gives (0, 3) in world space. The vector from the child origin to this point is one unit long.</p></details></aside>\n<p>During traversal, each child receives its parent's accumulated matrix. Compute the child's world matrix and pass it only to that child's descendants. When visiting the next sibling, start the calculation again from their shared parent.</p>\n<details class=\"sjekk\"><summary>Basic practice · Where is a child's origin in its own coordinate system?</summary><p>Distinguish the coordinate from the world position.</p><details><summary>Show answer</summary><p>The child's local origin is always (0,0,0). The transformation chain determines where that point lies in the world.</p></details></details>\n<div class=\"exercise\"><strong>Application · our practice exercise</strong><p>A parent is translated 5 units to the right. Two children have local displacements +2 and −3. An implementation reports world positions 7 and 4. Find the error.</p>\n<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>Which matrix was on top of the stack before the second child was visited?</p></details>\n<details><summary>Show answer</summary><p>The correct results are 5 + 2 = 7 and 5 − 3 = 2. The value 4 comes from 7 − 3. The program has allowed the first child's transformation to affect its sibling.</p></details></div>"
      },
      {
        "id": "referanse",
        "title": "Viewing one object from another",
        "source": "CGVIZ_Chapter_9_2020.pdf p. 8–12 · SceneGraphs.pdf § 9.2, PDF p. 4–6",
        "html": "\n<p>Let M<sub>A</sub> and M<sub>B</sub> map coordinates from A and B, respectively, to world space. To go from A to B, first go up to world space and then down using B's inverse: p<sub>B</sub> = M<sub>B</sub>⁻¹ M<sub>A</sub> p<sub>A</sub>. Shared ancestors cancel out.</p>\n<p>A lamp is at world position (4, 2). A camera at (1, 2) has the same axis directions. The lamp is at (4 − 1, 2 − 2) = (3, 0) in camera space. If the camera is also rotated, express the vector along the rotated camera axes.</p>\n<p>To express the same physical point in another reference frame, you need the placement of both frames. World space provides a shared intermediate frame. The inverse maps from world space into the target frame. A zero scale factor makes the general inverse mapping impossible.</p>\n<details class=\"sjekk\"><summary>Basic practice · In which direction does the inverse map?</summary><p>Assume B maps local coordinates to world space.</p><details><summary>Show answer</summary><p>B⁻¹ maps world coordinates to local B-coordinates. B⁻¹B is the identity when B is invertible.</p></details></details>\n<div class=\"exercise\"><strong>Application · our practice exercise</strong><p>Frame A has its origin at world-x = 4 and an x-unit twice as large as the world's. Frame B has its origin at −1 and the standard unit length. Find the B-coordinate of x = 3 in A.</p>\n<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>First calculate the world position. Then find the displacement from B's origin.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Show answer</summary><p>The world position is 4 + 2·3 = 10. The B-coordinate is 10 − (−1) = 11. Substitute back into B: −1 + 11 = 10.</p></details></div>"
      }
